# On-lattice kinetic Monte Carlo approaches for modeling molecular anisotropy in resveratrol crystallization

Tesia D Janicki, Tyler R Kennelly, Jonathan Leonard, Christine Roberts, Rekha R Rao, Theron M Rodgers

Sandia National Laboratories, Albuquerque, NM 87185, United States of America.

Corresponding author: tdjanic@sandia.gov.

_Modelling and Simulation in Materials Science and Engineering_ **33**, 055010 (11 pp), 2025.

DOI: 10.1088/1361-651X/ade176.

Received 27 February 2025; revised 12 May 2025; Accepted 5 June 2025; Published 18 June 2025.

###### Abstract

Stilbenes are a class of organic compounds with broad-ranging pharmaceutical and agricultural applications, which are typically isolated and purified through recrystallization. We are motivated by reducing experimental waste and optimizing yield via developing predictive simulations for processing-dependent crystal morphologies. Using resveratrol as a model stilbene system, we have developed an approach for simulating crystallization with molecular resolution using on-lattice kinetic Monte Carlo. In this work, we highlight modifications to the Stochastic Parallel PARticle Kinetic Simulator (SPPARKS) software package, which were essential to this application. Key enhancements include the incorporation of non-orthogonal cell shapes and monomer anisotropy approximations using bound hard spheres. This new SPPARKS application has been applied to resveratrol with attachment energy libraries obtained from density functional theory, resulting in excellent agreement with experimental morphology prediction.

**Keywords:** kinetic Monte Carlo, crystallization, resveratrol, stilbene.

## 1 Introduction

Stilbenes are a class of molecules with a (1,2)-diphenylethylene base structure with pharmaceutical, agricultural, and chemical-sensing applications. _trans_-resveratrol carries three hydroxyl groups and is used as an antioxidant dietary supplement and in cancer prevention/treatment. Processing conditions impact final crystal morphology, which is directly correlated with product properties. This work develops a workflow for characterizing crystallization in stilbenes using resveratrol as a specific example.

Kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC) uses binding energies derived from higher-accuracy methods (e.g. QM) to determine the probability of a monomer attaching to any site on the growing crystallite. The Stochastic Parallel PARticle Kinetic Simulator (SPPARKS) [27,28] is an open-source software package. To simulate surface attachment using kMC [28,33], SPPARKS randomly distributes deposition attempts uniformly over an assigned surface. The probability \(\Gamma\) of a monomer binding to site \(i\) determines whether the deposition is accepted (sticks) or rejected, according to the Arrhenius relation:

\[
\Gamma(i) = \nu \exp\!\left[ \frac{E_\text{bind}(i)}{k_\text{B}T} \right]  \tag{1}
\]

where \(T\) is temperature, \(k_\text{B}\) is Boltzmann's constant, \(E_\text{bind}(i)\) is tabulated by the coordination number of the monomer on site \(i\), and \(\nu\) has units of inverse time chosen from experimental precursor flow rate (gas) or concentration (liquid).

On-lattice models enable growth characterization for specific crystal polymorphs but typically assume that all monomers are isotropic. Prior to this work, molecular anisotropy had not been implemented in SPPARKS but was deemed essential for accurate representation of resveratrol. Rather than changing the shape of the monomer to a spherocylinder [34,35], we took inspiration from linear tangent hard sphere (LTHS) representations of molecule chains [36-38] and represented each resveratrol unit with two bound hard spheres. Deviating from LTHS models, binding was geometrically enforced by the lattice positions rather than applying an additional intra-site force. By reducing each 28-atom molecule down to two sites, this approximation makes on-lattice crystal growth tractable for resveratrol while retaining relevant physics within molecular geometry.

## 2 Additions to the SPPARKS package

### 2.1 Non-orthogonal lattice construction

The first addition to SPPARKS was enabling non-orthogonal simulation boxes [39]. Specific lattice sites for the HCP unit cell are enabled via `lattice HCP a`, where \(a\) is the a-lattice parameter. Remaining box vectors are defined as \(|\vec{a}|=|\vec{b}|=\sqrt{3/8}\,|\vec{c}|\) and \(\theta_{ab}=60^\circ\). This lattice style must be used in conjunction with the new `hex` region style, which accounts for unit cell replication and periodicity.

For parallel computations, hex subdomains are assigned on a cubic grid prior to shifting the atoms by the x-component of the y-lattice vector, `xylattice`. This new branch also permits 3D deposition: instead of defining an incident vector for particle deposition, the user may define the vector as (0 0 0), for which trial sites are generated randomly over all available surfaces.

Specific lattice sites for resveratrol are enabled in a second branch [40] and are invoked as `lattice resv 1.0`. Since the unit cell geometry is incorporated in this lattice type, the lattice parameter was set to unity.

### 2.2 Implementation for resveratrol

The second, more extensive addition to SPPARKS addressed the inherent anisotropy in stilbene geometry; this branch addition [40] was specific to P2\(_1\)/c resveratrol. Each monomer is represented by two bound spheres centered on the half-monomer center-of-mass and not permitted to dissociate. Accounting for the different functional groups on each half of the monomer, each sphere is permitted to bind only to specific sites within the lattice assigned to its specific number of OH substituents. Lattice style `resv` in this branch designates the resveratrol bound-sphere lattice configuration and must be used with `region hex`.

A new `diffusion/disphere` app style was created in the SPPARKS code to address bound-sphere motion. The binding energy table for the bound sphere approach uses 2D indexing for the coordination on each sphere in the bound pair. By consequence of being bound to a nearest neighbor, individual spheres must have a minimum coordination of unity. This app style currently can only support the resveratrol lattice.

### Table 1

Schematic of on-lattice kMC algorithm and modifications made for bound sphere kMC.

| Traditional kMC | Bound sphere kMC |
| --- | --- |
| 1. Generate random event for \(i \rightarrow j\) | 1a. Generate random event as if moving a single site \(i_1 \rightarrow j_1\); 1b. Identify sites coupled to \(i_1\) and \(j_1\) |
| 2. Determine initial and final coordination of site | 2. Determine initial and final coordination of each site in the disphere pair |
| 3. Look up associated energies for coordination assignments | 3. Look up associated energies |
| 4. Compute probability of this move, equation (1) | 4. Compute probability of this move, equation (1) |
| 5. Accept or reject | 5. Accept or reject |

## 3 Resveratrol simulation and experimental comparison

### 3.1 Energy library generation

Each binding energy was computed from the energy of a surface with a deposited monomer, less the energy of the pre-deposited surface and free monomer. Unique binding events were identified by first depositing single monomers from the unit cell then identifying a series of nearest-neighbor depositions. A total of 74 surface depositions were incorporated.

All energies were computed using all-atom density functional theory (DFT) with the Fritz-Haber Institute _ab initio_ materials simulation package (FHI-aims) [41] with 'light' numerical settings and tier-1 basis functions. Exchange-correlation interactions used PBE [42] with the Tkatchenko-Scheffler Hirschfeld-partitioning van der Waals correction [43] and a Pulay DIIS mixing algorithm [44] with 0.1 eV Gaussian broadening.

The periodic P2\(_1\)/c resveratrol unit cell [45] was optimized with fixed box shape, 3\(\times\)3\(\times\)1 k-point mesh, BFGS [46] with 0.01 eV Å\(^{-1}\) trust radius, and SCF convergence within 10\(^{-4}\) eV. A single gas-phase monomer was relaxed with identical settings.

Surface calculations were performed along each of the three facet planes of the unit cell with a >20 Å vacuum gap and bottom monomers fixed to mimic an infinite crystal.

### Table 2

Geometric parameters for facet-dependent deposition.

| Direction | Replication (a\(\times\) b\(\times\) c) | Monomer layers frozen |
| --- | --- | --- |
| (100) | 4\(\times\)3\(\times\)2 | 2 |
| (010) | 6\(\times\)2\(\times\)2 | 2 |
| (001) | 6\(\times\)4\(\times\)1.5\(^\text{a}\) | 1 |

\(^\text{a}\) To improve computational cost and account for monomer length in this direction, the bottom monomer from the unit cell was deleted; the second-to-bottom monomer was fixed.

DFT-generated energies were input as `ecoord` binding energies, indexed by local coordination of the two half-molecule center-of-masses. Coordinations not explicitly set from a DFT binding calculation were given an arbitrarily large barrier.

### 3.2 Simulating resveratrol crystal attachment in SPPARKS

Initial resveratrol lattices were generated using the new `resv` lattice type. The periodic simulation box was tuned during trial runs to accommodate variation in growth velocity for each facet. Unique crystal nuclei sizes and shapes listed in Table 3 were invoked for each of 5 simulations. Unless otherwise noted, the total boxsize for each production run encompassed a 48\(\times\)16\(\times\)24 periodic unit cell volume (~200 nm \(\times\) 250 nm \(\times\) 210 nm).

### Table 3

Initial crystal nuclei geometries for each run. Each run was seeded by a unique random number.

| RunID | Description | Replication (a\(\times\) b\(\times\) c) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Cube\(^\text{a}\) | 4\(\times\)1\(\times\)2 |
| 2 | Bigger cube | 8\(\times\)2\(\times\)4 |
| 3 | Short in _a_ | 4\(\times\)2\(\times\)4 |
| 4 | Short in _b_ | 8\(\times\)1\(\times\)4 |
| 5 | Short in _c_ | 8\(\times\)2\(\times\)2 |

\(^\text{a}\) box size replication set to 12 in the _b_-direction for this run.

The temperature was set at 20 °C in energy units as 0.0270 eV/\(k_\text{B}\). The `event` deposition style was invoked with a 0.1 s\(^{-1}\) prefactor, 5.0 Å incident capture distance, with depositions only permitted on sites with coordinations between 1-9. The `tree` solve style was used for 5\(\times\)10\(^{6}\) trial steps with statistics saved every 10\(^{5}\) steps.

We identified a strong degree of anisotropy in the trajectories, with growth proceeding most rapidly along a single direction, which agrees qualitatively with experimental observation.

### 3.3 Experimental validation

2 g resveratrol were dissolved in 20 g of 2:1 ethanol-water solution. The mixture was heated in an oven at 70 °C for 24 h. The solution was transferred to a chamber maintained at 20 °C and left undisturbed for 5 days.

Crystal structures were observed with Micro-CT (Bruker SKYSCAN 1272; 0.45 µm resolution) [47]. The reconstructed crystal population model was analyzed in Dragonfly v2024.1 [48], where individual crystals were segmented from the population via a watershed transform. 1207 crystals were segmented and exported as mesh files. Length \(L\) = longest axis, width \(W\) = shortest, height \(H\) = middle.

Figure 7 gives H:L and W:L aspect ratio distributions of experimental crystals and simulated results. Distributions for experiment come from independent crystallites within the solution, while simulation distributions come from 560 independent simulation replicants using different random number seeds. Simulated distributions are much narrower than experiment, but the distribution peaks overlap well.

## 4 Conclusions

We have introduced several modifications to the SPPARKS simulation package to probe crystal growth mechanisms in resveratrol: (i) non-orthogonal (hexagonal) simulation boxes have been enabled for the general HCP unit cell; (ii) bound-sphere motion enables non-spherical particles in on-lattice kMC models for crystal attachment. This new infrastructure was implemented for crystal attachment in resveratrol using DFT-based event libraries and shows excellent agreement with experiment for qualitative crystal morphologies.

